we turned to ethiopia where the  suffering continues after more than a  year after the government launched an  attack in the northern tigray region  rights groups say atrocities including  the mass rape of women and girls have  been committed by parties on both sides  the federal government is still  restricting media and humanitarian  access to degray  meanwhile regions bordering to cry are  on a war footing to grind fighters have  withdrawn back into their home region  but their neighbors are still full of  mistrust dw east africa correspondent  mario muller reports from the amhara  town of lalibela where people are taking  up arms  la libella's rocky churches look  peaceful in the morning light they seem  untouched by the events of the past  months  la bella has twice been captured by  fighters loyal to the tigray people's  liberation front or tplf the town has  now been liberated by government forces  here at bergeron the morning prayers  just ended  this church is one of 11 medieval  churches cut into rock  it has great symbolic value for all  ethiopians this is also why the tplf was  keen to conquer this town  at this time of year one of the holiest  for orthodox christians lalibella is  normally full of international visitors  and diaspora ethiopians  but most of them have stayed away  worried that fighters could swoop in  again from the neighboring tigray region  even though the tplf promised in  december to withdraw  all of their fighters back into tigre  [Music]  many people have fled  those who stayed here we suffered a lot  of stress  they pointed their guns at us the second  time they came they raped women and beat  and killed people  they looted into town and raped and beat  women they looted their hotels we had no  power and water  tigran fighters also attacked the town  of gashena not far from la bella  here we meet a man who doesn't wish to  be identified for fear of tplf  retaliation they killed his uncle he  tells us  and his brother-in-law  when 